diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** None — all subsystems are healthy at the time of investigation.

**Mechanism (why the claim is unconfirmed):** The user report asserts a leaf-to-spine link failure, but a link fault in a BGP CLOS fabric would manifest as one or more BGP sessions dropping out of the Established state. Every session in the fabric is currently Established: both leaf routers (ASN 65200, 65201) maintain two Established sessions each to the two spine routers; both spine routers (ASN 65100, 65101) maintain three Established sessions each to their leaf and super-spine peers; and the super-spine (ASN 65000) maintains two Established sessions to both spines. A persistent or recurring link flap would also leave a running flap process detectable by `pressure_sweep` — none was found.

**Localization:** End-to-end pings between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) succeeded with 0% loss and ~1.4 ms RTT in both directions. All 16 fabric interfaces carry only the default `fq_codel` qdisc (no shaping). No ACL drop rules, no duplicate MACs, no addressing errors, and no resource pressure were found on any of the 7 devices.

**Root cause:** There is no active or evidenced past fault — the dc_clos_bgp fabric is fully operational and the reported connectivity loss is unconfirmed by any live or historical trace.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the outage was genuinely observed earlier, collect BGP session logs or syslog from the leaf/spine routers during the reported window to identify any transient event.
=== END ===